GIT THEORY

DevOps Training Program

Prepared By: Sathvik Jakka
Topic: Version Control Concepts and Git Fundamentals
Duration: 45 Minutes

Introduction

In modern software development, multiple developers work together on the same application. Without a proper system to manage code changes, developers may overwrite each other's work, lose important code, or struggle to track modifications.

Version Control Systems (VCS) were created to solve these problems. Git is the most popular Version Control System used today and has become an essential tool in DevOps, Software Development, Cloud Engineering, and Site Reliability Engineering (SRE).

Git helps developers track code changes, collaborate efficiently, maintain code history, and safely manage software releases.

What is Version Control?

Version Control is a system that records changes made to files over time so that previous versions can be recalled whenever needed.

Think of Version Control as a "save history" for software projects.

Instead of keeping multiple files such as:

Project_Final.zip
Project_Final_V2.zip
Project_Final_V3_Updated.zip

Git maintains all changes in an organized way and allows developers to move back to any previous version.

Benefits of Version Control
Tracks every code change
Maintains project history
Allows collaboration among developers
Prevents accidental loss of code
Enables rollback to previous versions
Supports parallel development
Why Do Software Companies Use Git?

Almost every software company uses Git because software development involves continuous changes and collaboration among multiple team members.

Git helps organizations:

1. Collaboration

Many developers can work on the same project simultaneously without affecting each other's work.

2. Change Tracking

Every modification is recorded with details such as:

Who made the change
When the change was made
What was changed
3. Backup and Recovery

Git stores project history, making it easy to recover deleted or modified files.

4. Faster Development

Developers can create separate branches for new features and work independently.

5. Release Management

Different versions of software can be maintained and released efficiently.

6. DevOps Integration

Git integrates with:

Jenkins
GitHub Actions
GitLab CI/CD
Docker
Kubernetes
Terraform

This makes Git a core component of modern DevOps pipelines.

What Problems Does Git Solve?

Before Git, developers faced many challenges.

Problem 1: Lost Code

Developers often accidentally deleted or overwrote important code.

Git Solution

Git keeps a complete history of all changes and allows restoration of previous versions.

Problem 2: Multiple Developers Editing the Same File

Two developers might edit the same file at the same time.

Git Solution

Git identifies conflicts and helps merge changes safely.

Problem 3: No Backup

If a system crashed, all project files could be lost.

Git Solution

Code can be stored remotely on platforms like GitHub, providing backup and recovery.

Problem 4: Difficulty Tracking Changes

Teams could not easily identify who modified the code.

Git Solution

Every commit stores author information and change details.

Difference Between Git and GitHub

Many beginners think Git and GitHub are the same, but they serve different purposes.

Git	GitHub
Version Control System	Cloud Hosting Platform
Installed on local machine	Hosted online
Tracks code changes	Stores Git repositories
Works offline	Requires internet for remote operations
Created by Linus Torvalds	Owned by Microsoft
Example

Git is like Microsoft Word on your computer.

GitHub is like Google Drive where you store and share files online.

Together they help developers manage and collaborate on software projects.

What is a Repository?

A Repository (Repo) is a storage location where project files and Git history are maintained.

A repository contains:

Source code
Configuration files
Documentation
Commit history
Branch information
Types of Repositories
Local Repository

Stored on the developer's computer.

Remote Repository

Stored on platforms such as GitHub.

Example

Restaurant Website Project

Repository Structure:

Restaurant-Website/
├── index.html
├── style.css
├── script.js
└── README.md

All files and history are stored inside the repository.

What is a Commit?

A Commit is a snapshot of the project at a specific point in time.

Whenever developers complete a task, they save the changes using a commit.

Example Commit Messages
Added login page
Fixed payment bug
Updated navigation menu
Added Docker configuration
Importance of Commits
Tracks development progress
Creates project checkpoints
Allows rollback if issues occur
Improves team collaboration

A commit acts like a save point in a video game. If something goes wrong, developers can return to an earlier commit.

What is a Branch?

A Branch is an independent line of development within a Git repository.

Branches allow developers to work on new features without affecting the main codebase.

Default Branch

Most repositories use:

main

or

master

as the primary branch.

Example

Main Branch
|
|---- Feature Login
|
|---- Feature Payment
|
|---- Feature Search

Each feature can be developed separately and later combined into the main branch.

Benefits of Branching
Safe experimentation
Parallel development
Easier collaboration
Better release management
What is Merge?

Merge is the process of combining changes from one branch into another branch.

After a feature is completed and tested, it is merged into the main branch.

Example

main
|
|---- feature-login

Developer completes login functionality.

Then:

feature-login → main

The login feature becomes part of the main application.

Benefits of Merging
Combines completed work
Maintains project consistency
Supports team collaboration
What is a Pull Request?

A Pull Request (PR) is a request to merge code from one branch into another.

Before merging, team members review the code to ensure quality and correctness.

Pull Request Workflow

Developer Creates Branch
↓
Writes Code
↓
Creates Pull Request
↓
Code Review
↓
Testing
↓
Approval
↓
Merge to Main Branch

Benefits of Pull Requests
Code quality improvement
Team collaboration
Knowledge sharing
Bug detection before release

Pull Requests are widely used in GitHub-based development workflows.

Conclusion

Git is one of the most important tools in modern software development and DevOps. It helps teams manage source code, track changes, collaborate efficiently, and maintain project history. Concepts such as repositories, commits, branches, merges, and pull requests form the foundation of Git workflows used by organizations worldwide.

Understanding Git fundamentals is essential for anyone pursuing a career in DevOps, Cloud Computing, Software Development, Site Reliability Engineering, or Platform Engineering. Mastering Git improves productivity, collaboration, and software quality while supporting efficient CI/CD and automation practices.